<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiiumaa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dagö</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiiumaa is the second-largest island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, lying in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> north of Saaremaa, and known in German and Swedish as Dagö. Conquered by German crusaders in the 13th century, it belonged to the Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and from 1710 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and had a Swedish-speaking community until the late 18th century. Its Kõpu lighthouse, first lit in 1531, is among the oldest working lighthouses in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hiiumaa.jpg" alt="Hiiumaa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Blizzard1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaade_tahkuna_tuletornist4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hiiumaa covers 989 square kilometres and lies 22 km from the Estonian mainland, in the West Estonian archipelago of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. Its relief is low, reaching 68 m above sea level, and it is formed mostly of limestone, exposed in cliffs along parts of the coast. The Soela Strait separates it from Saaremaa to the south and the Muhu Strait from the mainland. Its names include Dagö in Swedish, Dagden in German and Hiidenmaa in Finnish. The island rose from the sea about 8,500 years ago through isostatic uplift after the ice cap retreated, and the land is still rising.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> settlements on the Kõpu Peninsula date from about 5500 BCE and seem to have been tied to seal hunting. Because the land has risen, they now lie far from the coast. Stone-cist graves on the island span the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> to the Late Iron Age.</p><p>The island is first recorded as Dageida in 1228, when it and the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> were conquered by Germanic crusaders. In 1254 it was divided between the Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek and the Livonian branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, acting partly for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><p>The oldest surviving church, at Pühalepa, was built in 1259, although it was rebuilt in the 18th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> built a lighthouse at Kõpu in the 16th century. As Dagö the island belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-estonia">Swedish Estonia</a> and later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, whose rule left the Swedish population most of its privileges. Most of the numerous Swedish speakers later emigrated or were Estonianised, though a minority remained into the 20th century.</p><p>German forces occupied Hiiumaa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and in 1918 it became part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> occupied it in 1940, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1941 and the Soviet Union again in 1944. Until Estonia regained independence in August 1991 the island was closed to foreigners and to most of mainland Estonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hiiumaa is the main island of Hiiu County, and its largest town is Kärdla. Tourism, livestock, farming and fishing support its economy. The natural environment is protected in the Tahkuna Nature Reserve and the West Estonian Archipelago Biosphere Reserve. A ferry links the island to the mainland, and in winter an ice road can cross the frozen sea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hiiumaa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Läänemaa, known in Latin as Rotalia or Maritima, covered the western coast of Estonia facing the islands of Saaremaa and <b>Hiiumaa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/laanemaa">Läänemaa</a></li></ul></section>
